00:08:50.289 --> 00:08:57.490
OK, so let's start off by having a look at two of the most common nodes that we're going to use.

115
00:08:58.399 --> 00:09:01.039
And the first one is the selector.

116
00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:09.710
Now, how a selector works is, I think we have to have a sequence.

117
00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:11.470
You know, you can have a sequence.

118
00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:15.309
OK, so we have a selector and we have a sequence.

119
00:09:15.309 --> 00:09:19.789
Now, these function in a slightly different way to each other.

120
00:09:19.789 --> 00:09:27.149
So what a selector is going to do is it is going to look at all of the tasks below it.

121
00:09:28.049 --> 00:09:34.690
And any task below it can provide two outputs.

122
00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:41.250
It can either succeed or it can fail, once the task has basically hit its completion part.

123
00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.889
Now, I'm talking about selectors and sequences, too.

124
00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:51.299
But if something under a selector fails, it's going to go instantly back.

125
00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:58.100
So let me start off with a sequence. Maybe a sequence makes more sense.

126
00:09:58.100 --> 00:10:06.110
So in a sequence, say I'm going to create a couple of different tasks here.

127
00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:16.480
So, see, we were creating an AI very simply that was going to mimic a standard office job.

128
00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:22.639
You might have a sequence for the day, and at the beginning, you would have the function of

129
00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:36.960
get up, then you would have get dressed, and then we would have go to work,

130
00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:40.379
and maybe finally we would have work.

131
00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:48.179
And this could go on, but this is enough for our example.

132
00:10:50.220 --> 00:11:00.019
Now, in this case, because this is a sequence, we would have to do all of these things

133
00:11:00.019 --> 00:11:02.899
in order for this to be considered a success.

134
00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:08.110
So, for example, you couldn't get dressed if you hadn't got up.

135
00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:10.590
If you weren't successful at getting up, you could not get dressed.

136
00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:14.110
If you haven't got dressed, it's not recommended for you to go to work.

137
00:11:14.669 --> 00:11:20.509
And if you haven't gone to work, it's probably you would be able to work.

138
00:11:20.509 --> 00:11:23.950
So you can see how these things have to go in a sequence.

139
00:11:23.950 --> 00:11:29.870
And if any of these fails, the entire work sequence has failed.

140
00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:33.470
If you fail to get dressed, if you fail to get up, if you fail to go to work,

141
00:11:34.029 --> 00:11:35.870
then work has failed.

142
00:11:36.620 --> 00:11:37.899
So that's all the sequences.

143
00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:43.740
One by one, all of these have to succeed for us to send this back as a success.

144
00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:48.539
If at any point any of these fail, the whole sequence has failed.

145
00:11:49.679 --> 00:12:00.659
Now let's change go to work to be a selector, and we'll call it go to work again.

146
00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:11.659
But the way a selector works is you may have multiple different tasks below it.

147
00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:12.860
I'm going to drag off.

148
00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:18.700
And so I'm going to take sequences just so I can.

149
00:12:20.980 --> 00:12:24.019
So going to work, you might drive to work.

150
00:12:25.179 --> 00:12:27.100
So your first thing to do is drive to work.

151
00:12:28.669 --> 00:12:37.340
The second thing to do would be take the bus.

152
00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:43.950
And the third thing to do is walk to work.

153
00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:53.649
Now, the way a selector is different is if you go to work,

154
00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:58.289
if you attempt to drive to work, if that is successful,

155
00:12:58.289 --> 00:13:00.129
you're not going to move on to the next thing.

156
00:13:00.129 --> 00:13:03.090
You're going to move on to the next thing in the sequence up here.

157
00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:07.169
So the whole thing is going to be a success if you manage to drive to work.

158
00:13:07.809 --> 00:13:11.809
However, if you fail to drive to work,

159
00:13:11.809 --> 00:13:14.450
the selector is then going to try the second thing.

160
00:13:15.009 --> 00:13:16.129
It isn't going to give up.

161
00:13:16.129 --> 00:13:18.529
It isn't going to report it all as a failure.

162
00:13:18.529 --> 00:13:20.370
It's going to attempt to take the bus.

163
00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:24.929
So say the car doesn't work, then you try and take the bus to work.

164
00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:28.210
And if that succeeds, then go to work has succeeded.

165
00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:30.049
And we can go back to the work option.

166
00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:35.120
Third one is if the bus fails, then we could walk to work.

167
00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:40.159
And if you successfully walk to work, then you also get to work.

168
00:13:41.059 --> 00:13:46.559
But the main thing is this is only considered a failure

169
00:13:46.559 --> 00:13:49.600
if all of the options below it have faith.

170
00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:55.649
And so if any one of the options below it succeeds,

171
00:13:55.649 --> 00:13:57.090
you've officially gone to work,

172
00:13:57.090 --> 00:13:59.570
and you can start on the next part of the sequence.

173
00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:05.779
So hopefully this helps explain the progress of it is

174
00:14:05.779 --> 00:14:09.299
that a sequence will have to go in order.

175
00:14:09.299 --> 00:14:12.740
If any one of them fails, the entire sequence has failed.

176
00:14:13.299 --> 00:14:16.580
The selector will select the furthest to the left.

177
00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:20.419
If that fails, it will try the next one, and then the next one,

178
00:14:20.419 --> 00:14:26.259
and it will only be marked as a fail if the entire of this section has failed.

179
00:14:28.019 --> 00:14:32.179
Okay, there's one more thing that I think is worth noting.

180
00:14:32.179 --> 00:14:34.179
And for that, I'm going to zoom in a bit.

181
00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:36.980
I'm going to connect up this sequence here.

182
00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:43.009
You can see that this has numbers next to each of them.

183
00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:47.009
And that's important because, and it's important to recognize that

184
00:14:47.009 --> 00:14:49.250
because within the rest of Unreal,

185
00:14:51.009 --> 00:14:57.409
the location of the nodes placements usually doesn't matter.

186
00:14:57.409 --> 00:14:59.090
The connection matters.

187
00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:03.649
But in this case, if I was to drag and get dressed before get up,

188
00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:06.610
you can see that this has now got a one on it,

189
00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:09.250
and get up has got a two on it.

190
00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:16.269
So the placement affects the order that things work in.

191
00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:23.950
And our path is always going to go down the left most node first.

192
